In 1899, the unit was expanded with a second battalion, formed primarily to allow the regiment to serve outside the protectorate.{{sfn|Baker|2001|p=79}} The two battalions were also known as the First and Second Central African Rifles.{{sfn|Boeder|1981|p=72}}{{sfn|Campbell|1986|p=8}} The new battalion was soon given its first overseas assignment, being sent to [[British Mauritius|Mauritius]] to act as a garrison force.{{sfn|Marjomaa|2003|p=422}} Eight companies were sent, totalling seven European officers, 32 Sikhs and 878 African soldiers, accompanied by 220 wives and 77 children.{{sfn|Marjomaa|2003|p=423}} The deployment was a failure due to abuse from the local population and outbreaks of bronchitis and pneumonia amongst the troops, their wives and their children, blamed on warm clothing not being issued.{{sfn|Boeder|1981|p=72}} Nearly 30 of the contingent died.{{sfn|Marjomaa|2003|p=423}} Despite this, as Sharpe remarked, the soldiers maintained "remarkable discipline."{{sfn|Boeder|1981|p=72}} On 5 July, [[Queen Victoria]] declared that the regiment be named the Central Africa Regiment.{{sfn|Naval & Military Intelligence|1899|page=10}} On 1 August, Colonel [[Willoughby Verner]] led a detachment of the First Battalion in what was termed the Anglo-Portuguese Nquamba and Mataka Expedition.{{sfn|Verner|1906|p=59}} The force of ten British officers, 135 Sikhs, 500 African soldiers, a Maxim gun and two 7-pound guns was deployed alongside Portuguese troops against the Yao chief Mataka. Although mainly acting in support of the Portuguese, it was the Central Africa Regiment that burnt the town of Nquamba.{{sfn|Baker|2001|p=78}}

At the same time, the British Empire increasingly saw the [[Mullah]] of [[British Somaliland|Somaliland]], [[Muḥammad ibn 'Abdallāh Hassan]], as a threat.{{sfn|Beachy|1990|p=38}} He was declared an outlaw; his [[Dervish movement (Somali)|Dervish fighters]] attacked Indian merchants, and he stated that would "drive the British back into the sea whence they had come".{{sfn|Beachy|1990|p=40}} The Second Battalion was transferred from Mauritius to Somaliland, the first detachment arriving in Berbera at the end of February 1900.{{sfn|Stacke|1941|p=551}}{{sfn|Boeder|1981|p=72}} The climate in Somaliland proved beneficial to the troops, and they quickly recovered and trained.{{sfn|Naval & Military Intelligence|1899|page=10}}{{sfn|The 2nd Battalion Central Africa Regiment|1900|page=4}} On 15 June, the battalion was made aware that a French supply caravan, disguised as a shooting expedition, had left Djibouti with arms for the Dervishes. A force of 60 soldiers of B Company surprised and captured the party, scoring the first operational success for the Second Battalion.{{sfn|Moyse-Bartlett|2012 Volume 1|page=32}}

Meanwhile, there had erupted a conflict between the British Empire and the [[Asante Empire]] in what became the [[War of the Golden Stool]]. On 19 June, a contingent of four officers, 73 Sikh troops and 276 African soldiers of the First Battalion, along with a medical officer, hospital and machine gun detachment, departed from Zomba, embarking on the transport ''[[SS Russian (1895)|Victorian]]'' on 30 June with 750 carriers.{{sfn|Page|2011|page=3}}{{sfn|Boeder|1981|p=72}} On 9 July, it was also decided to send half the Second Battalion to the conflict.{{sfn|Hall|1939|p=141}}{{sfn|Marjomaa|2003|p=423}} The force, including 70 Sikh and 200 African troops, sailed via [[Cape Town]] on 11 July to West Africa.{{sfn|''Correspondence relating to the Ashanti War''|1901|p=45}}{{sfn|The Infantry of East Africa Command|1944|p=6}} The four companies arrived at the former slave station [[Cape Coast Castle]] on 12 August.{{sfn|The 2nd Battalion Central Africa Regiment|1900|page=4}}

On 13 August, two companies, joining troops of the West African Rifles and supported by two 7-pound mountain guns, marched for [[Kumasi]], arriving the following day.{{sfn|Armitage|Montanaro|1901|pp=164, 165}} The force successfully defeated the Asante troops at the stockade, the regiment suffering seventeen wounded. The Sikh troops were particularly praised for their bravery by the British command,{{sfn|Armitage|Montanaro|1901|pp=168, 169}} From there, the British force, including the Second Battalion, marched to [[Esumeja]] to defeat the Asante army commanded by Queen [[Yaa Asantewaa]], but the leaders fled or surrendered as they advanced.{{sfn|Hall|1939|pp=328, 332}} A large detachment of four companies, 70 Sikh and 200 African soldiers, of the First Battalion arrived on 20 August.{{sfn|Stacke|1941|p=551}}{{sfn|Armitage|Montanaro|1901|p=173}} Together, these forces undertook forays into nearby towns and villages, including the religious centre of [[Ejisu]], that routed the remaining Asante fighters.{{sfn|Armitage|Montanaro|1901|p=178}} Following the defeat and death of the Asante leader Opoto on 23 August, on 1 September the advance to Kumasi was complete.{{sfn|The 2nd Battalion Central Africa Regiment|1900|page=4}} The battalion's A Company was the first to reach the capital. The battalion fought in the battle of Obassa on 30 September, achieving a decisive victory that led to the end of the war.{{sfn|Hill|2006|page=44}}

Back in British Somaliland, in mid-September, while on patrol near the Ethiopian border, a contingent of the regiment under [[Lieutenant colonel|Lieutenant Colonel]] J. S. Graham observed the Mullah in camp unprepared. However, in honour of an agreement not to cross the border, they did not attack.{{sfn|Beachy|1990|p=41}} On 4 October, the Yao of the Central Africa Regiment were the only force of the British flank that stood their ground in a battle in [[Mudug]].{{sfn|Beachy|1990|p=47}} This proved critical to the battle, which finished with a British victory.{{sfn|Beachy|1990|p=48}}

By December 1900, the deployment of the Second Battalion in British Somaliland had mainly ceased.{{sfn|Beachy|1990|p=42}} The remaining half of the Second Battalion departed Somalia for the [[Gambia Colony and Protectorate]].{{sfn|Stacke|1941|p=551}} An uprising in the colony had started on 14 June following the deaths of two British officials in [[Sankandi]].{{sfn|''The London Gazette''|1901|p=5973}} On 2 January 1901, the force arrived and were accommodated at Bathhurst, present-day [[Banjul]], on 10 January.{{sfn|''The London Gazette''|1901|p=5974}} On the following day, two companies of the battalion participated in the successful capture of [[Dumbutu]], suffering casualties of two wounded soldiers in the fight, and, on 15 February, three companies were deployed to [[Fogny]] and [[Kombo]], meeting no resistance.{{sfn|''The London Gazette''|1901|pp=5975, 5976}} Subsequently, the soldiers were deployed to villages along the frontier. This campaign was the only time that troops of the regiment were commanded by naval officers.{{sfn|Moyse-Bartlett|2012 Volume 1|page=46}} Following a successful campaign, on 30 March, the companies returned to the [[Gold Coast (British colony)|Gold Coast]] and were reunited with the remainder of the Second Battalion.{{sfn|''The London Gazette''|1901|p=5977}}

In June that year, a detachment of one Sikh and twenty African troops, including six [[sergeant]]s, three [[corporal]]s, twelve [[Private (rank)|privates]] and an interpreter, travelled to England.{{sfn|Boeder|1981|p=73}} They were inspected by the [[Commander-in-Chief of the Forces]], [[Frederick Roberts, 1st Earl Roberts|Frederick Roberts]], and were presented to [[Edward VII]] at [[Marlborough House]], who bestowed them with the [[Ashanti Medal]] and [[Africa General Service Medal]] for their service in the war with the Asante and in the Gambia.{{sfn|Page|2011|page=4}}{{sfn|Moyse-Bartlett|2012 Volume 2|page=698}} This was the first visit by Malawians to England.{{sfn|Boeder|1981|p=73}}

On 1 January 1902, the regiment was merged with the [[East Africa Rifles]] and [[Uganda Rifles]] to form the [[King's African Rifles]]. The two battalions of the Central Africa Regiment became the 1st and 2nd Battalion.{{sfn|''The Infantry of East Africa Command''|1944|p=6}}

==Description==
===Local recruitment===
[[File:Sikh contingent of the British Central African Rifles 1898.png|[[Sikh]] members of the regiment in 1898|alt=Eight soldiers standing abreast with shouldered rifles|thumb]]
The British had gained much experience with the use of non-British troops across the empire by the time of the foundation of the Central Africa Regiment, particularly in the conquest of India and the foundation of the [[British Raj]]. The use of companies of different ethnic groups, serving out of the native territories, had become well tested.{{sfn|Marjomaa|2003|p=418}} The emerging territory of British Central Africa contained many different ethnic groups and the British decided to found companies based on ethnic lines. It was hoped that this would foster comradeship in the unit, encourage competition between units and also reduce the risk of different ethnic groups combining against their colonial rulers. The original structure of the force had envisioned three of the initial six companies coming from the Tonga due to their longer experience of British culture via missionaries.{{sfn|Marjomaa|2003|p=419}} This did not come to pass. Although a small cohort joined in 1895 as regular troops, they only signed up for a year and for local service. Instead, the majority of the first recruits were Yao, with 50 enlisting in that year alongside 25 [[Chewa people|Chewa]].{{sfn|Baker|2001|p=69}} They signed up for three years of service.{{sfn|Marjomaa|2003|p=419}}

The Tonga were generally more educated, and so the more aspirational members of the community served in administrative positions in the government rather than join the army. At the same time, the lack of plantations in the Yao territories meant that they had more freedom to move and so could more easily enlist. The collapse of the chiefdoms amongst the Yao had also led to the release of a large number of trained musketeers that were looking for work and who were particularly attracted to a career in the nascent army.{{sfn|Marjomaa|2003|p=420}}

===Commissioned and non-commissioned officers===
Initially, there was no recruitment of officers and non-commissioned officers for the Central African Rifles from the local people. Leadership was recruited from amongst the experienced officers of the Indian Army.{{sfn|Campbell|1986|p=8}} All the officers came from Europe and the non-commissioned officers came from Europe and India.{{sfn|Marjomaa|2003|p=421}} Initially, each company was commanded by a British officer and had a Sikh [[colour sergeant]], with three to four Sikhs taking on leadership of a [[Section (military unit)|section]], which each contained a number of African troops, the remaining one filling the role of [[drill sergeant]]. Sikhs also fulfilled other roles, including quartermaster. Although the language barrier could have caused problems, this was overcome by what historian Colin Baker calls, based on the testimonies of [[John Gough (British Army officer)|John Gough]] and Claude Percival, [[Captain (British Army and Royal Marines)|captains]] that served in the Rifles, "the keenness of the Malawian soldiers to excel."{{sfn|Baker|2001|p=69}}  The officers were also often those that had served in active roles in the Indian Army, particularly at Zomba.{{sfn|Baker|2001|p=72}}

In October 1898, this situation changed. Following an unsuccessful counterattack following a raid that led to twenty Malawian women and children civilians being abducted, the regiment decided that it would no longer be necessary to have a Sikh leading a section. The standing order requiring a Sikh to accompany any troop of the Central Africa Regiment in service was cancelled, and African soldiers were promoted to non-commissioned ranks.{{sfn|Baker|2001|p=76}} It was thought that a company that only consisted of African troops was more mobile, a proposition immediately vindicated as the company based in Mangoche, where the cancellation initially applied, swiftly overtook the raiders and rescued the captives.{{sfn|Verner|1906|pp=56–57}} Non-commissioned officers were recruited from the sons of chiefs and other leaders and increasingly also took on the role of instructors from their Sikh colleagues.{{sfn|The British Central Africa Protectorate|1899|page=13}}

At the same time, the balance in the African troops had changed. Initially the regiment consisted of six companies; A Company, D Company and E Company were nominally recruited from the Tonga, B Company and C Company were Yao and F Company was a combination of Chewa and Ngoni.{{sfn|Baker|2001|p=69}} Conditions of service were initially limited to the protectorate, but, in 1898, this was altered and up to two years could be spent abroad.{{sfn|Marjomaa|2003|p=422}} By this time the number of companies had increased to eight, with G Company another Tonga company and H Company consisting of Yao soldiers.{{sfn|Baker|2001|p=70}}

===Uniform and equipment===
Initial recruits had a uniform that followed the standard of the Indian Army, which consisted of a turban, white shirt, cut-away open-fronted jacket, [[cummerbund]], [[Knickerbockers (clothing)|knickerbocker]]s and [[puttee]]s. The English officers were kitted out in red and the Indians in yellow. The jacket was replaced by a loose khaki shirt when on active service. Each of the [[other ranks]] was issued with two uniforms. For commanding officer's parades and military action, the soldiers wore a khaki coat, loose khaki [[shorts]], blue puttees and a black [[Fez (hat)|fez]], which had a tassel.{{sfn|Moyse-Bartlett|2012 Volume 2|page=689}} This was renewed every year. For other purposes, a blue cotton uniform was provided with a red fez at enlistment. Any damage to this was the responsibility of the soldier. They were also provided with brown belts, two pouches, a khaki rucksack and a water bottle.{{sfn|Baker|2001|p=75}} The soldiers of the Central Africa Regiment were called "the smartest and most soldier-like body of men" of those serving in the west coast of Africa by the [[Governor of the Gambia]], [[George Chardin Denton]].{{sfn|''The London Gazette''|1901|page=5973}} The regiment had a contingent of musicians that played the [[Corps of drums|drums and fife]], known as the "canary birds" after their uniform of bright yellow trousers.{{sfn|Verner|1906|p=47}}

The troops were originally issued with [[Snider–Enfield]] rifles, but, in 1899, these were replaced with the [[Martini–Enfield]]. Although a superior weapon, its use initially led to a drop in accuracy as the soldiers found it hard to adapt to the [[smokeless powder]] it used.{{sfn|Baker|2001|p=79}} By 1902, with training and experience, the soldiers were proving more accurate, and the use of the Martini–Enfield was deemed, in the words of Captain Gough, an "unqualified success".{{sfn|Baker|2001|p=80}} During the War of the Golden Stool, the soldiers fixed [[bayonet]]s to their rifles and used these to charge the enemy, which was often sufficient to break the Asante line and cause them to retreat without a shot being fired.{{sfn|Armitage|Montanaro|1901|p=177}}

===Life in the army===
Discipline in the army was harsh, and flogging was common. Although [[corporal punishment]] had been banned across most European armies, it was still considered acceptable to flog African troops.{{sfn|Marjomaa|2003|p=421}} Unlike in many contemporary armies, the soldiers were allowed to bring wives and children with them within the garrison, married men receiving private quarters while the rest slept in dormitories. This was considered particularly favourable, as traditionally a man had to ask permission of the elders to marry, which could be circumvented if the man married while a serving soldier.{{sfn|Marjomaa|2003|p=422}} The policy was to rarely move officers so they understood the local conditions well and invested in their quarters to make them as comfortable as they could.{{sfn|Baker|2001|p=72}}{{sfn|Verner|1906|p=51}} The regiment achieved a high level of cohesion and operational efficiency in the battlefield, particularly under fire. In a 1900 report to [[Reuters]], the regimental medical officer Douglas Gray said, "The Central African Rifles never wavered in the slightest degree" and "constitute one of the most highly-trained and well-disciplined native forces in the empire".{{sfn|The Native African Regiments|1900|page=11}}

==Summary of name changes==
* 1896 – British Central African Rifles.{{sfn|Stacke|1941|p=550}}
* 1898 – Central African Rifles.{{sfn|Lewis|2002|p=72}}
* 1899 – Central Africa Regiment.{{sfn|Njoloma|1998|page=19}}
* 1900 – 1st and 2nd Central African Rifles.{{sfn|Campbell|1986|p=8}}
* 1902 – 1st and 2nd Battalion King's African Rifles.{{sfn|Marjomaa|2003|p=423}}

